    "result_text": "Terms now count business days, and `bin/e2e` passes all 4 tests in the spec.\n\n**How due-by works now**\n- Each claim stores the terms it was fixed with and the date the counting starts from (the claim's date when it was added).\n- Due-by is the terms-th business day after that start date, skipping Saturdays, Sundays and the sheet's holidays. The counting is in a new helper, `claim/BusinessDays.java`.\n- When a claim is added or split, `ClaimService` works out its terms the same way as before (the person's own terms, else their grade's, else the sheet's) and then counts business days. The old \"move past a holiday\" step (`pastHolidays`) is gone.\n- When a holiday is added, `HolidayService.add` works out every claim's due-by on that sheet again against the holidays now. It then writes ROLLUP/TOTALS audit lines for any figures that changed, the same way setting changes already do.\n- Overdue, the late charge's 30-day boundaries and which period a claim belongs to all read the stored due-by, so they follow without further changes.\n\n**Changing a claim's date doesn't move its due-by.** Editing or re-dating a claim keeps the original start date. The spec expects this: a claim re-dated to 2026-02-25 keeps due-by 2026-03-04. Counting from the new date would give 2026-03-02.\n\n**Stored data**\n- A new migration, `V53__claim_business_day_terms.java`, adds the two columns. It's written in Java because counting business days in SQL is awkward.\n- For claims already in the database, it takes their terms to be the calendar days from date to due-by, minus any holidays the old due-by had been pushed past. It then works out their due-by again as business days.\n- The database is in-memory and starts empty on every run, so this conversion hasn't been exercised against real data.\n\n**Other changes**\n- The \"Terms (days)\" labels on the person and settings forms now say \"Terms (business days)\".\n- Claims restored for the as-of view keep the due-by saved in the snapshot. Snapshots don't record terms, so those restored claims carry 0 terms; that value isn't used for anything there.",
